Close the /proc/<pid>/attr/* AND setxattr(security.selinux) hidden-type oracles
on 6.6.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

6.6 sits between the two shapes and used to fall to the wrong side of the split.
Its selinux_inode_setxattr() is already the mnt_idmap-era one that
hide_selinux_attr_6_12.patch guards, but its selinux_setprocattr() is still the
6.6 shape, not the selinux_lsm_setattr() the 6.12 variant is fitted to. So the
builder pinned the setprocattr-ONLY fallback on 6.6, and

	setxattr(f, "security.selinux", "u:object_r:ksu_file:s0")

-- a second, independent one-syscall probe of the same bit -- stayed uncovered
there. The README named 5.10/5.15/6.1 as the versions with that gap and left 6.6
out of the list, so the gap was real, recorded in two files and stated in
neither.

This variant carries BOTH guards for 6.6: the fallback's setprocattr hunk and
the 6.12 variant's inode_setxattr hunk, byte-for-byte. It was GENERATED by
applying those two hunks to the real android15-6.6 tree and diffing the result,
so the type list and the uid gate cannot drift from the files it was cut from --
which matters more here than anywhere else in this directory, because the list
now lives in four places instead of three.

COVERAGE, RESTATED SO THE TABLE IS COMPLETE

	5.10 5.15 6.1   hide_selinux_attr.patch       setprocattr only
	                                              -> setxattr STILL uncovered
	6.6             hide_selinux_attr_6_6.patch   setprocattr + inode_setxattr
	6.12            hide_selinux_attr_6_12.patch  lsm_setattr  + inode_setxattr

5.10/5.15/6.1 remain uncovered for setxattr and that is not an oversight: their
selinux_inode_setxattr() takes neither a struct mnt_idmap * nor a struct
user_namespace * in the shape the 6.12 hunk expects, so covering them needs a
third hunk shape fitted and dry-run against those three trees. Reachability
there is narrow -- the guard sits after FILE__RELABELFROM, which no AOSP app
domain holds on its own files -- which is why it has stayed open, not because
nobody noticed.

PLACEMENT

Both guards sit AFTER the stock avc check (FILE__RELABELFROM, and the per-attr
PROCESS__SET* check respectively) and immediately before
security_context_to_sid(), so a domain lacking the permission still gets the
stock EACCES and never EINVAL. See common/_hook/README.md for why that ordering
is the whole design and what breaks when it is inverted. Both scans are bounded
by strnlen(value, size) -- exactly the bytes security_context_to_sid() will look
at -- for the trailing-junk reason the same README records.

APPLIES TO

android15-6.6 (the pinned OnePlus tree) and, incidentally, 6.12 -- where
hide_selinux_attr_6_12.patch must still win, because it additionally guards
selinux_lsm_setattr(), which lsm_set_self_attr(2) reaches directly. Selection is
BY NAME per kernel version in scripts/apply_nomount_stack.sh, which is exactly
why an overlap like this is safe here and would not be under "first that
applies".

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on the pinned OnePlus trees: applies on
6.6 and 6.12, FAILS on 5.10, 5.15 and 6.1. Both guards were then confirmed to
land inside the functions they name.

